  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid level ridge will retrograde west into the Southern Plains with \nmore of a northwesterly flow developing across the Ohio Valley. \nSurface front stalls south of the area overnight as surface high \npressure builds east across the northern Great Lakes/southern Canada.\nThere will be a brief respite in precipitation tonight into Saturday\nmorning. Light northeast winds, decreasing clouds, and lingering low\nlevel moisture will likely lead to fog development overnight - most\nimpactful in valley locations.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nAny fog that develops overnight will improve Saturday morning. A low\nlevel jet of 25-30 kts will offer favorable theta-e advection. This return\nflow of moisture will provide an axis of moderate instability over \neastern Indiana, western Ohio, and northern Kentucky. CAM solutions \nshowing storms developing to our northwest and increasing in coverage \nas they track southeast thru the area during the afternoon hours.\nEffective shear is limited to around 20 kts so do not expect great\norganization to storms. DCAPE values approaching 1000 J/KG pointing\nto the potential for strong to damaging winds. Machine learning\nguidance is also focused on the Tri-State region for strong to\ndamaging winds. PWAT values increase above 2 inches - so heavy rain\nand localized flooding is also a threat. Have bumped up pops and \nwill message these threats in HWO products.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3)\n\nAn active band of westerlies will exist across the region thru early\nnext week. An unsettled weather pattern continues thru mid week as\nmultiple shortwaves will track through the Ohio valley in the Sunday\nthru Tuesday timeframe before a change in the weather pattern is \nobserved the middle of next week. Air mass is very moist with\nanomalously high PWAT values around 2 inches. With convection near \na quasi-stationay boundary there will be the potential to produce \nrepeated rounds of showers and storms. This along with high \nPWAT values will lead to the potential for heavy rain and flooding.\n\nAs a mid level low moves into the area at mid week some lingering\nshows will be possible but temperatures will turn cooler.",
